.Dd November 16, 2026
.Dt PATCH 1
.Os RavenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm patch
.Nd apply unified diffs (bounded strict applier)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Ruf
.Op Fl p Ar strip
.Op Fl i Ar patchfile
.Op Ar patchfile ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility applies unified diffs to files named by the
.Sq ---
and
.Sq +++
headers.
Patch text is read from
.Fl i Ar patchfile ,
from the patch-file operands in order, or from standard input when neither is present.
.Pp
Every hunk is checked before a file is changed.
Context and removed lines must match at the expected offset.
There is no context fuzz: a hunk that does not match rejects the whole invocation and no file is
written or removed.
.Pp
The following options are supported:
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Fl i Ar patchfile
Read the patch from
.Ar patchfile .
A single
.Sq -
means standard input.
.It Fl p Ar strip
Strip
.Ar strip
leading slash-separated components from the target path.
The default is 0.
.It Fl R
Apply the patch in reverse, swapping the old and new sides.
.It Fl u
Accept unified input.
Unified diffs are the only supported format, so this is a no-op.
.It Fl f
Force mode.
Accepted for command-line compatibility; it does not relax hunk matching.
.El
.Pp
A file is created when the old side is
.Pa /dev/null
or the first hunk has a zero-length old range.
A file is deleted when the new side is
.Pa /dev/null .
Paths containing a
.Sq ..
component are rejected.
Header timestamps separated from the path by a tab are ignored.
.Pp
Only unified diffs are supported.
Context diffs, ed scripts, binary patches, backup files, reject files, interactive prompts and
file-mode changes are not implemented.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Status 2 indicates invalid usage; status 1 indicates a malformed patch, a missing target, a
non-applying hunk or an I/O error.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr diff 1 ,
.Xr grep 1 ,
.Xr sed 1
.Sh STANDARDS
The bounded core follows the POSIX.1-2024
.Xr patch 1
unified-diff model, with the documented strict-matching limitation.
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
utility first appeared in 4.3BSD.
